Parallel execution-context checks
=================================

  ./maxima-local --no-init --lisp=sbcl \
    --batch-string='run_testsuite(tests=[rtest_parallel_lifecycle]);'

Run every configured Lisp and the complete core+share suite. The 28 appended
examples preserve all previous expected values and problem numbers. Check
the full count and diagnostic output even when Maxima exits zero.

*PARALLEL-EVALUATION-P* is an internal special, NIL outside the scheduler's
execution scopes. CALL-AS-RUNNER binds it to T after applying captured
bindings, for native workers, caller participation and the serial fallback.
Nested calls preserve their enclosing scope; normal return and unwinding
restore the caller's value. Input restrictions have a separate flag.

A package adapter can call (ENSURE-SERIAL-EXECUTION '$operation) before an
operation that requires serial execution. The helper returns NIL in ordinary
serial use and signals a localized Maxima error in a parallel scope. This
is an explicit guard, not a new user option or a mathematical algorithm.

Tests force each execution site, inspect exact guard error data and verify
that a guarded body's side effects are not reached. They cover conflicting
captured values, nested regions, caller errors/nonlocal exits, empty jobs,
outer true/false values, and independence from the interactive-input flag.
A reproducible generator exercises 32 nested job trees with varying depths,
widths and worker limits, checking exact integer squares at their leaves.

The flag describes CALL-IN-PARALLEL execution. Bare threads created by
external Lisp code need their own coordination. It is not a certification
that every unguarded operation is safe to run concurrently.
